<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yemọja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yemaja, Yemanjá, Yemayá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yemọja is a major water deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion, the patron spirit of rivers, above all the Ogun River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and of the oceans in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. She is said to be motherly and protective, the protector of women and the one who can cure infertility, while her anger is likened to the floodwaters of turbulent rivers. Some of her priests hold that she lent her fresh water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Ọbàtálá</a> as he moulded human beings from clay. Many devotees depict her as a mermaid, and since the era of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> she has been identified with Our Lady of Regla and other figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yemoja.jpg" alt="Yemọja" width="330" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manu Herbstein · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abeokuta_procession.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Her name is a contraction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> words iye, a dialect form of ìyá, mother; ọmọ, child; and ẹja, fish. Roughly rendered as mother of fish children, it expresses the breadth of her motherhood and fertility and her rule over all living things. In traditional Yoruba belief she is a mother spirit and the patron of women, especially pregnant women. Besides the Ogun River, other rivers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> are dedicated to her, and she is worshipped at almost any stream, creek or spring, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>.</p><p>Among the river deities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>, with Ọ̀ṣun, Erinlè̩, Ọbà and Yewá, she is counted a leader, while the role of sea deity belongs to Olókun. In some beliefs, when her waters broke they caused a great flood that made the rivers and streams, and the first mortal humans came from her womb.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Carried across the Atlantic, her worship changed its emphasis: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> she is a high-ranking river deity, but in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> she is honoured mainly as a deity of the sea. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> and Umbanda in Brazil, Yemanjá is one of the seven Orixás, the Queen of the Ocean, patron of fishermen and of survivors of shipwreck, and the spirit of moonlight. Her day is Saturday, her colours light blue and crystal, and white roses are offered to her. In Brazil she is also identified with Our Lady of Navigators and Our Lady of the Conception.</p><p>In Cuban Santería, or regla de ocha, Yemayá is the mother of all living things and owner of the oceans and seas. She has many roads, or aspects, among them Okute, Asesú, Achabá and Mayelewo, all marked by blue; her number is seven, and her necklace has seven blue and seven crystalline <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Her festivals draw large crowds. Every 2 February in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, thousands bring flowers, perfume, combs and mirrors to her shrine at Rio Vermelho, and fishermen carry the offerings out to sea. On New Year's Eve millions of Brazilians of all religions, dressed in white, throw flowers into the sea for her. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> she is honoured on 7 September with a procession in Regla, a tradition begun by the cabildos of enslaved people, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> worshippers launch small boats of offerings from Ramírez Beach every 2 February.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yemọja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…mechanics; Shango, the orisha of thunder, lightning and justice, armed with a double-headed axe; Oshun, the goddess of the river at Osogbo, of sweet water, love and fertility; <b>Yemoja</b>, mother of many orisha and goddess of the waters; and Oya, goddess of wind, storms and the Niger river.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
